<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#typhus">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Typhus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Typhus is a group of bacterial infectious diseases, often spread by lice, that cause fever and can be highly fatal in crowded or wartime conditions. It was among the Eurasian diseases introduced into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> after European contact. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">Smallpox</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/measles">measles</a> and typhus killed an estimated 50–90 percent of the indigenous American population within a century of contact.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/typhus.jpg" alt="Typhus" width="300" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: D. Raoult and others · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epidemic_typhus_Burundi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
